            <ArticleTitle>Activities of Proline-Specific Proteinases in the Serum and Cerebrospinal Fluid of Rats with the Fetal Valproate Syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>605-607</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10517-017-3859-y</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In 60-day-old Wistar rats with fetal valproate syndrome, the brain to body weight ratio was higher by 9.4% and activity of dipeptidyl peptidase IV in the serum and cerebrospinal fluid was higher by 18.4 and 40.6%, respectively, than in healthy controls. Activity of prolylendopeptidase in the serum and cerebrospinal fluid in rats with the fetal valproate syndrome did not differ from the control.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
